---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import { env } from "cloudflare:workers";
import { loadAccountNavigation } from "@/server/account-navigation";
export const prerender = false;
const account = await loadAccountNavigation(
  env,
  Astro.request,
  Astro.response.headers,
);
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy policy | After Hours Outreach Society"
  description="How After Hours Outreach Society handles information on its public website and volunteer portal."
  noindex
  privatePage
  account={account}
>
  <section class="bg-black px-6 py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="text-blue">Privacy policy</h1>
      <p class="mt-6">Effective October 27, 2026.</p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        After Hours Outreach Society ("we", "us") operates this website and its
        volunteer portal. This policy describes information handled through
        those services. It is not a complete policy for offline outreach
        records, donations, social media, or other activities outside the
        portal.
      </p>
      <h2 class="mt-12">Information we collect and why</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 list-disc space-y-4 pl-6">
        <li>
          <strong>Accounts and contact details.</strong> Your email address identifies
          your account, verifies sign-in and email changes, and receives service messages.
          Account records include your name, role, verification status, and creation
          and update times. Application names, optional pronouns, and phone numbers
          support identification, communication, and volunteer coordination. Your
          full date of birth is stored to check the application requirement that volunteers
          be at least 19.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Emergency and safety information.</strong> An emergency contact’s
          name, phone number, and relationship to you help organizers contact someone
          if you need help. Optional medical, allergy, or other safety information
          helps organizers support you during events. Share only what is relevant
          to that purpose. Please tell your emergency contact about this use and obtain
          their agreement before providing their details.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Application and qualifications.</strong> Team interests, motivation,
          training, experience, and any required medic certification and expiry date
          support application review, role suitability, and event safety. Referral
          answers help us understand how applicants find us. Training choices include
          potentially sensitive lived experience in the DTES, with substance use,
          or in recovery; you do not have to select that choice or describe that personal
          history. Organizers can see these answers after submission.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Submission records.</strong> New applications keep an original copy
          of the submitted answers and account email, the submission time, and the
          Code of Conduct version and acknowledgement time. Later profile edits do
          not replace that original copy. These records document the application and
          acknowledgement; accepting the Code of Conduct is not consent to unrelated
          uses of personal information.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Events and access.</strong> We record event signups, cancellations
          and moves, orientation completion, application and patrol approvals, account
          activity status, and role changes. Records of the person making a change
          and its time support coordination, eligibility, and accountability. Notification
          records can include recipients, subjects, message text, delivery attempts,
          and status.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Discord connection.</strong> With your explicit consent we link
          one Discord ID and username to your volunteer account, join our server,
          verify membership, and synchronize Volunteer, Organizer, and Medic roles
          from website eligibility. We keep consent and completion times, membership
          checks, and synchronization status/retry records. Discord linking is required
          after Naloxone 101 and before review for new applications; existing volunteers
          keep website access without linking. Email sign-in is unchanged.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Technical and authentication records.</strong> Hosting receives
          request information such as IP addresses and browser headers to serve the
          site. Authentication records include session identifiers, IP addresses,
          browser/device information, verification records, and hashed abuse-limiting
          counters. If you set up an authenticator, we store its setup secret, backup-code
          records, verification status, and failed-attempt information. These support
          sign-in and abuse prevention. Operational logs and traces support troubleshooting.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6">
        The application requires first and last names, phone number, birth date,
        emergency contact details, Outreach team selection, training and
        experience selections (which can be "No relevant training" and "No
        relevant experience"), motivation, referral selection, and Code of
        Conduct acknowledgement. Medic applicants must provide certification and
        expiry details. Selecting "Other" can require an explanation. Middle
        names, preferred name, pronouns, medical information, and additional
        team interests are optional. Without the required answers, the form
        cannot be submitted; organizer approval is needed before most volunteers
        can access events.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12">Drafts, cookies, and this browser</h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Collection starts before you submit an application. As you enter
        answers, the form backs them up in this browser’s local storage and
        automatically saves a draft to your signed-in account so you can resume
        on another device. This includes any medical, emergency-contact, or
        lived-experience answers you enter. Drafts are not available in
        organizer views, but are stored on our service as well as your device.
        Submission makes your profile available for organizer review.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Inactive drafts are not automatically deleted. Draft answers stay saved
        until you submit or discard them, we remove them under the retention
        schedule below, or a separate deletion request is handled. Browser
        copies on offline devices require separate attention.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Successful submission removes the account draft and clears the browser
        draft on that device. "Discard application draft" requests removal of
        your unsubmitted account draft and this browser’s draft answers; check
        any failure or retry message. It does not delete your account or a
        submitted application. Other devices may still hold local copies until
        they reconnect and check the draft’s status. Discard markers without
        answers remain to prevent old tabs from restoring discarded drafts.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Signing out clears browser draft copies whose answers have reached your
        account, but leaves unsynced backups on the device to avoid data loss.
        Signing out does not delete your account draft. Avoid shared devices;
        clear this site’s browser data on each device if you need to remove
        local copies. Clearing browser data alone does not delete server
        records.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Necessary authentication cookies keep you signed in and support email
        links and two-factor sign-in. Session cookies are HttpOnly (not readable
        by page scripts) and use the Secure setting on HTTPS. Sessions are
        configured for 30 days and renew with use. Blocking these cookies
        prevents portal sign-in. Browser draft storage is separate from cookies
        and can remain after a session ends.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12">Who can access volunteer information</h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        You can view your own account and, where captured, your original
        submitted application. Authorized active organizers with completed
        applications can review full current profiles, including emergency
        contacts, optional medical information, and training or lived-experience
        answers, to review applications and manage volunteering. Access is not
        limited to an organizer assigned to your event. Full-profile views and
        profile edits are audited; edit logs record changed field names, not
        copies of the answers.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Event rosters are organizer-only. They show volunteer display names
        (preferred names when provided) and orientation-completion status, not
        medical or emergency-contact answers. Other volunteers see available
        event details and signup counts, not other volunteers’ roster names or
        full profiles. Two-factor authentication is optional for both volunteers
        and organizers; once enabled, it is required at sign-in.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        People administering our hosting, database, and email systems may have
        technical access beyond the portal’s organizer screens. Service
        providers process information as described below. Any disclosure for an
        emergency or legal requirement must have an applicable legal basis and
        be limited to the information needed; this is not permission for
        unrestricted sharing.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12">Hosting, email, and external services</h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        The hosted service is configured to run on Cloudflare Workers and use
        Cloudflare D1 for account, authentication, profile, application-draft,
        event, audit, and notification records. Cloudflare also provides the
        hosting infrastructure for requests, logs, and traces. Our database
        currently runs in Western North America without a jurisdiction
        restriction. Request processing and provider support may involve other
        countries. Do not assume information stays in British Columbia or
        Canada.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        On our Workers Free plan, Cloudflare provides seven days of D1 recovery
        history. Its documented retention is three days for stored Workers logs
        and seven days for stored traces. These windows do not set how long our
        application keeps profiles, application drafts, or audit records, and do
        not describe every category of provider security or account record.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Hosted sign-in and email-change messages use Resend, which receives the
        recipient address and message content, including the verification code
        and, for sign-in, a sign-in link. Event, role, and access-change emails
        also use Resend when an organizer chooses to send them. Their content
        can include event details, access status, and an organizer-entered
        reason. Production medic-certification reminders include the expiry date
        listed in the profile. Notification content and delivery records are
        also stored in D1. Do not put sensitive answers in emailed reasons.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        We use Resend Free. Resend states that customer data is stored in the
        United States, email and log data is retained for 30 days, and backups
        persist for seven days. Open and click tracking are disabled for our
        sending domain. Earlier deletion of a specific message can be requested
        through Resend support. Provider retention does not remove copies
        already delivered to recipients’ inboxes.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        In production, first application submissions automatically queue a
        minimal email to three fixed organizer inboxes:
        ivan.zheng@afterhoursoutreach.ca, info@afterhoursoutreach.ca, and
        molina.dewilde@afterhoursoutreach.ca. Resend receives the applicant’s
        display name (preferred name, or full name when none is provided),
        submission time, and an organizer-only review link, not the other
        application answers. The link requires authorized organizer sign-in and
        grants no access by itself. D1 keeps the fixed recipient, message
        content, delivery attempts, and status, including unresolved deliveries
        that can no longer be retried safely. Provider acceptance is not
        confirmation of inbox delivery. Draft saves, submission retries, and
        later profile edits do not create another notification. Local
        development simulates these messages; preview builds suppress them
        without storing message content or sending to the organizer inboxes.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Discord authorization requests only identity and permission to join our
        server, not your Discord email, messages, contacts, or other servers.
        The dedicated bot receives your Discord ID and the eligibility-based
        role changes; we do not send profile answers, medical details, or
        emergency contacts to Discord. Server members may see your membership
        and assigned roles, including Medic or Organizer status. Discord
        processes this information under its own
        <a
          href="https://discord.com/privacy"
          class="underline underline-offset-4">privacy policy</a
        >, potentially outside Canada. We store the join access token encrypted
        only until joining/membership verification succeeds or the token
        expires; refresh tokens are not retained. Role sync uses the private bot
        credential, not a retained user access token. A ten-minute HttpOnly
        browser cookie and single-use authorization record protect the
        connection process.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Deactivation or revoked application approval removes all three synced
        roles; reactivation restores eligible roles. Other roles and server
        membership are preserved. Leaving the server does not automatically
        rejoin you without fresh consent. Contact us to correct a link, remove
        it, or withdraw consent; an organizer must first arrange removal of
        synced roles, then remove the link. We retain the link and minimal
        synchronization state while needed for these purposes, subject to the
        retention schedule and decision-record requirements below. Discord-side
        records require separate handling.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Some public pages connect to Shopify’s image CDN, including donation
        product images; those requests disclose connection information such as
        your IP address to that provider. Donation and social links lead to
        separate services such as GoFundMe and Discord, whose own practices
        apply when you visit them. This policy does not describe those services’
        records or all information the Society may receive outside this portal.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Local development uses a local database and simulated emails, not real
        email delivery. Development account-switching tools can bypass normal
        sign-in challenges for local accounts. Use sample answers only in
        development and preview testing.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12">Safeguards and limitations</h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        The portal uses authenticated, role-checked access, organizer
        profile-view auditing, profile-edit logs, and restrictions on caching
        and third-party resources on account pages. These measures reduce risk
        but do not guarantee security. Keep sign-in links, codes, and
        authenticator backup codes private, and contact us if you suspect
        unauthorized access. Our Privacy officer coordinates privacy requests,
        complaints, and the response to suspected privacy incidents. Organizers
        must use volunteer information only for authorized volunteer-management
        and safety purposes and keep it confidential.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12">How long we keep information</h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        We keep personal information only while needed for the purposes
        described in this policy or a documented legal or operational
        requirement, then delete or anonymize it subject to the retention
        exceptions below.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Our Privacy officer is responsible for administering the following
        schedule through manual reviews and deletion, as well as handling
        individual requests through the contact below. There is no general
        automated cleanup or self-service account deletion. The periods below
        are our retention rules, not a claim that deletion happens
        automatically.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-6 list-disc space-y-4 pl-6">
        <li>
          <strong>Unsubmitted drafts:</strong> delete draft answers after 30 days
          without an answer edit. Browser copies on offline devices cannot be remotely
          erased and need separate handling when the site is next used.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Accounts without a submitted application:</strong> delete after
          90 days without sign-in. The shorter draft-answer period still applies.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Volunteer profiles:</strong> keep while needed for active participation.
          Review after 24 months without sign-in or event participation and give 30
          days’ notice before deleting an inactive profile. Keep only information
          still required for a documented legal or operational purpose, with a further
          review date.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Event and audit records:</strong> normally keep identifiable event,
          attendance, profile-view, edit, and access-change records for 12 months
          after the event or action. Keep the minimum current orientation and eligibility
          records while needed for active participation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Decision records and legal holds:</strong> preserve information
          actually used for a decision directly affecting a person for at least one
          year after each use, as required by BC’s Personal Information Protection
          Act. This minimum, and any documented legal hold, overrides routine deletion.
          Review any need for longer retention. An original application snapshot or
          field-name audit log does not by itself capture every later decision’s supporting
          records.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Technical and provider records:</strong> the provider windows above
          are separate from our application’s retention. We retain authentication
          records only while needed for sign-in, account security, abuse prevention,
          or a documented investigation. Expired verification records and abuse counters
          are cleaned up during sign-in housekeeping; expiring a code or session is
          not proof that every related record has been erased. We review notification
          content and delivery history for deletion once they are no longer needed
          for delivery, safe retries, investigation, or required decision records.
          Minimal records needed to prevent duplicate sends or restoration of discarded
          drafts may need to remain after message content is removed.
        </li>
        <li>
          Copies in other browsers, provider systems, email inboxes, or backups
          may require separate handling. Clearing one copy does not establish
          that all copies are gone. Recovery and backup windows are not a
          promise of immediate or universal erasure. A backup restore also needs
          to account for previously approved deletions.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-12">Requests, corrections, and complaints</h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Contact the Privacy officer at After Hours Outreach Society at <a
          href="mailto:info@afterhoursoutreach.ca"
          class="underline underline-offset-4">info@afterhoursoutreach.ca</a
        > with privacy questions or a written request for access, correction, deletion,
        or withdrawal of consent where consent applies. You can also ask how your
        information has been used or disclosed, or raise a privacy complaint. Identify
        the request and the account email involved; do not send sign-in codes, authenticator
        secrets, or unnecessary medical details. Identity may need to be verified
        before information is released or changed.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Our Privacy officer monitors this inbox and handles requests manually,
        keeping a private record of the request and response. We verify identity
        through an established account or contact channel and agree a suitable
        way to provide sensitive information. Access and correction requests are
        generally due a response within 30 business days under BC’s Personal
        Information Protection Act, subject to permitted extensions. We explain
        any extension or refusal and the available review options. We do not
        charge for access to volunteer employee personal information.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        You can edit ongoing profile details in <a
          href="/volunteer/account"
          class="underline underline-offset-4">Your account</a
        > and change your email through verification. Legal names, birth date, and
        the original submission cannot be changed through the profile editor; contact
        us about corrections. Corrections to retained original submissions need separate
        handling rather than only changing the current profile. There is no self-service
        account deletion. We explain the outcome of deletion or consent-withdrawal
        requests, including any information that must be kept, why, and the effect
        on participation. These requests are not a promise of immediate erasure.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Our Privacy officer investigates privacy complaints. You may also
        contact the <a
          href="https://www.oipc.bc.ca/for-the-public/"
          class="underline underline-offset-4"
          >Office of the Information and Privacy Commissioner for British
          Columbia</a
        > about access or privacy concerns, including a complaint you cannot resolve
        with us.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12">Policy updates</h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        We will publish updates here and notify affected account holders by
        email before material changes take effect. Where required, we will
        obtain fresh consent before using information for a new purpose.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
